nodecode-vault is a rewrite of Guerilla-ops's Guerilla-ops/floorvault:

  https://github.com/Guerilla-ops/floorvault
  commit 62e41853d1d9bf4d84f3ff22d3755d9762efdd3c
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Scott Lee (LICENSE)

The original is plugins/hermesvault, a Hermes Agent secret-source plugin: a
local AES-256-SIV store of agent secrets whose items are named hv:<item> or
hv:<item>:<field>, mapped from the harness's configuration to environment
variable names, with the key held apart from the store (an OS key store, a
key file or a systemd credential), plus an operator CLI (setup, add, put,
show, rm, list, env, doctor) and command bindings enforced against the
command line the harness was started with. What was rewritten in Common Lisp:
the store and its refs, the mapping from variable names to refs, the key
custody by key file and by environment variable, and the operator's verbs as
/vault plus the (vault:...) functions, with the model-facing half the
original had none of -- five tools, of which vault_run is the equivalent of
the original's fetch: it puts the mapped variables into a command's
environment instead of into the harness's. The seal is AES-256-GCM from
ironclad rather than AES-256-SIV, because ironclad is the crypto system the
serving image already carries; per-item encryption, one key per store and
the whole file rewritten on every change are as the original has them.

What was left out, and why: the Python floorvault library and its SQLite
store (the cell is one sealed file, so nothing has to be installed); the OS
key-store and systemd-creds custody, which need platform bindings a cell in
this image does not carry, so the key is a 0600 file or an environment
variable and an operator who wants a service manager to hold it points
key_env at what that manager exports; the command bindings enforced against
the harness's own argv, which have no meaning for a cell that resolves on
demand; the browser-fill login vault the original deliberately keeps apart,
which it never read either; and the CLI's prompted `put` and `--stdin`
fields, since /vault takes the value on its own line or the model passes it.
